    <header class="masthead">
      <p class="kicker">THE COMED LETTER &middot; ILLINOIS POWER &amp; DATA CENTERS</p>
      <h1>The forecast finally caught up</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        A year ago this newsletter looked at PJM&rsquo;s load forecasts
        for northern Illinois and concluded:
        <em>&ldquo;the flat prediction is probably wrong.&rdquo;</em>
        Twelve months of new data are in. The prediction was wrong
        &mdash; and PJM now agrees, loudly, in its own numbers.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="body">
      <h2>The scoreboard</h2>
      <p>
        The chart below is the whole story. Each thin gray line is a
        PJM annual forecast &ldquo;vintage&rdquo; for the ComEd zone
        (published 2016&ndash;2024); the thick blue line is what
        northern Illinois plants actually generated, from PUDL&rsquo;s
        cleaned EIA records. For most of a decade, every new vintage
        forecast <em>less</em> demand than the one before it, while
        actual generation quietly climbed past all of them.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <svg id="fan" width="960" height="420" role="img"
          aria-label="ComEd actual generation vs PJM forecast vintages"></svg>
        <figcaption id="fan-caption">
          ComEd-zone annual generation (GWh): PUDL actuals vs PJM
          forecast vintages. Actuals are the county-filtered
          approximation defined in the original analysis.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Then the correction arrived, in two installments. The
        <strong>2025 vintage</strong> (orange) broke the decade-long
        pattern: for the first time since 2016, PJM forecast ComEd
        demand meaningfully <em>above</em> its previous vintage &mdash;
        about 104,800 GWh for 2028, versus the 91,400 the 2023 vintage
        had penciled in. The <strong>2026 vintage</strong> (violet)
        went further: <strong>133,800 GWh for 2030 &mdash; 47% above
        what the 2023 vintage projected for the same year</strong>.
        The &ldquo;AI bump&rdquo; that was missing from every forecast
        we reviewed in 2025 is now the dominant feature of the
        forecast.
      </p>

      <h2>Grading the September 2025 observations</h2>
      <ol class="grades">
        <li><span class="grade pass">HELD</span>
          <strong>&ldquo;Past generation shows a steady, modest
          rise.&rdquo;</strong> Still true, and less modest: ComEd-zone
          actuals reached ~109,400 GWh in 2025, up from ~105,000 in
          2023.</li>
        <li><span class="grade pass">HELD</span>
          <strong>&ldquo;Hyperscaler growth is not an illusion; states
          and consumers are feeling the price increases.&rdquo;</strong>
          PJM&rsquo;s capacity auction for 2026/27 cleared at a record
          $329.17/MW-day &mdash; up roughly elevenfold from $28.92 two
          auctions earlier &mdash; with data centers driving most of
          the increase. Illinois&rsquo;s consumer advocate now calls
          data-center rate reform urgent.</li>
        <li><span class="grade pass">VINDICATED</span>
          <strong>&ldquo;PJM forecasts do NOT show an expected AI bump
          and are relatively flat.&rdquo;</strong> True of every
          vintage available to the 2025 analysis (2016&ndash;2023).
          See below for the plot twist about the vintages that
          weren&rsquo;t available.</li>
        <li><span class="grade pass">CALLED IT</span>
          <strong>&ldquo;The flat prediction is probably
          wrong.&rdquo;</strong> PJM has since rewritten its ComEd
          outlook upward twice, and its own 2026 report leads with
          significant long-term load growth.</li>
      </ol>

      <aside class="confession">
        <h3>A confession about the &ldquo;corrupt&rdquo; file</h3>
        <p>
          The 2025 analysis noted that PJM&rsquo;s 2024 forecast data
          file &ldquo;was corrupted and could not be included.&rdquo;
          It wasn&rsquo;t corrupt. PJM&rsquo;s 2024 and 2025 workbooks
          carry document properties whose XML the standard Python
          reader (openpyxl) refuses to parse; a more tolerant reader
          (calamine) opens them fine. Which means the file we
          couldn&rsquo;t open last year was the one containing
          PJM&rsquo;s first admission that demand was about to bend
          upward. The forecast gods have a sense of humor.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>Forecast accuracy, re-scored</h2>
      <p>
        MASE (Mean Absolute Scaled Error) compares each vintage against
        actuals over the months where both exist; below 1 beats a
        na&iuml;ve forecast, above 1 loses to it. The deterioration the
        2025 analysis flagged has continued: the 2023 vintage now
        scores <strong>1.96</strong>, the worst on record. The young
        vintages score better so far &mdash; but with 17 and 5
        overlapping months respectively, those grades are
        provisional.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <svg id="masebars" width="700" height="220" role="img"
          aria-label="MASE score by forecast vintage"></svg>
        <figcaption>MASE by vintage, recomputed with actuals through
          May 2026 using the original method. Lower is better.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The mix, one more year on</h2>
      <p>
        The generation-mix picture that anchored the original analysis
        extends another year without changing its character: nationally,
        gas dominant and growing, coal fading, renewables grinding
        upward; in Illinois, nuclear still towering over everything
        else. The last few months are provisional in EIA&rsquo;s data
        and typically revise upward.
      </p>
      <div class="mix-row">
        <figure>
          <svg id="mix-us" width="470" height="260" role="img"
            aria-label="US monthly generation by fuel"></svg>
          <figcaption>US monthly net generation (TWh)</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <svg id="mix-il" width="470" height="260" role="img"
            aria-label="Illinois monthly generation by fuel"></svg>
          <figcaption>Illinois monthly net generation (TWh)</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="legend" id="fuel-legend"></div>

      <h2>The map</h2>
      <p>
        Illinois power plants (sized by cumulative generation, colored
        by primary fuel) and the data centers from the original
        survey. The geography still tells the policy story: nuclear
        and data centers cluster in ComEd territory up north; the big
        fossil fleet sits downstate.
      </p>
      <div id="map" class="map-box" role="application"
        aria-label="Illinois plants and data centers"></div>

      <h2>Receipts</h2>
      <p class="cite-note">
        Fresh citations supporting the year-old call, all
        2025&ndash;2026:
      </p>
      <ul class="cites">
        <li><a href="https://insidelines.pjm.com/pjms-updated-20-year-forecast-continues-to-see-significant-long-term-load-growth/">
          PJM Inside Lines: Updated 20-year forecast continues to see
          significant long-term load growth</a> (Jan 2026) &mdash;
          PJM&rsquo;s own framing of the 2026 vintage.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.pjm.com/-/media/DotCom/library/reports-notices/load-forecast/2026-load-report.pdf">
          PJM 2026 Load Forecast Report</a> (Jan 14, 2026) &mdash; the
          source of the violet line above.</li>
        <li><a href="https://insidelines.pjm.com/pjm-auction-procures-134311-mw-of-generation-resources-supply-responds-to-price-signal/">
          PJM capacity auction results</a> &mdash; the record
          $329.17/MW-day clearing price for 2026/27.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.canarymedia.com/articles/data-centers/pjm-record-capacity-costs-rising-bills">
          Canary Media: PJM&rsquo;s capacity costs hit record as grid
          falls short on supply</a> &mdash; data centers drove most of
          the roughly tenfold price increase.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.citizensutilityboard.org/blog/2026/07/15/cub-sustained-high-pjm-capacity-prices-ramp-up-urgency-for-data-center-reform/">
          Illinois Citizens Utility Board</a> (Jul 2026) &mdash;
          sustained high capacity prices make data-center reform
          urgent for Illinois ratepayers.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/governors-push-more-sway-over-biggest-us-grid-power-bills-surge-2025-09-22/">
          Reuters: Governors push for more sway over PJM as power
          bills surge</a> (Sep 2025) &mdash; carried over from Vol. 1;
          the politics have only escalated.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Methods, unchanged on purpose</h2>
      <p class="methods">
        Everything above was recomputed with the <em>same transforms</em>
        as the September 2025 analysis so the grades are comparable:
        PUDL&rsquo;s cleaned EIA tables (now through May 2026),
        the same fuel-category mapping, the same 26-county
        approximation of the ComEd zone, the same MASE definition, and
        PJM&rsquo;s published COMED forecast sheets (now eleven
        vintages, 2016&ndash;2026). The original medallion pipeline
        &mdash; Prefect, dbt, DuckDB, Grafana, and the full Databricks-free
        writeup &mdash; is documented in the
        <a href="https://github.com/rbhughes/power_puddle">repo</a>,
        with the refresh script
        <a href="https://github.com/rbhughes/power_puddle/blob/main/src/scripts/build_site_data.py">alongside
        it</a>. The frozen September 2025 database is kept as-is; this
        page never overwrites its baseline.
      </p>
    </article>

    <footer>
      <p>
        Data: PUDL (Catalyst Cooperative) EIA compilations; PJM load
